What:		/sys/class/nvme_mdev/nvme_mdev/mdev_supported_types/nvme_mdev-V1/
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	linux-nvme@lists.infradead.org
Description:
		The NVMe mediated device type: a virtual NVMe PCIe controller
		whose commands are executed by the NVMe target (nvmet), see
		the nvmet/ attributes.  While an instance is open it has its
		own polling I/O thread, "nvme-mdev-io", which is a thread of
		the process that opened the device (e.g. QEMU): its CPU time
		and I/O are accounted to that process and its cgroups, and it
		starts out with that process's CPU affinity (change it with
		sched_setaffinity(2), e.g. taskset -p).  Writing a UUID to
		"create" makes a new instance (see sysfs-bus-vfio-mdev); at
		most 16 instances exist at a time ("available_instances").

What:		/sys/bus/mdev/devices/<uuid>/nvmet/portid
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	linux-nvme@lists.infradead.org
Description:
		(RW) The nvmet port the instance uses: the number of its
		configfs directory, /sys/kernel/config/nvmet/ports/<portid>.
		The port must have transport type "pci" and be enabled (a
		subsystem linked to it).  0 by default.  Can only be changed
		while the device is not open (-EBUSY otherwise).

What:		/sys/bus/mdev/devices/<uuid>/nvmet/subsysnqn
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	linux-nvme@lists.infradead.org
Description:
		(RW) The NQN of the nvmet subsystem whose namespaces the
		guest gets.  When the device is opened, an nvmet controller
		is created for it on the port above; the subsystem must allow
		the instance's host NQN (see hostnqn).  Empty by default:
		the device can't be opened until it is set.  Can only be
		changed while the device is not open (-EBUSY otherwise).

What:		/sys/bus/mdev/devices/<uuid>/nvmet/hostnqn
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	linux-nvme@lists.infradead.org
Description:
		(RO) The host NQN of the instance's nvmet controllers,
		"nqn.2014-08.org.nvmexpress:uuid:<uuid>" (their host ID is
		the instance's UUID too), for the subsystem's allowed_hosts.

What:		/sys/bus/mdev/devices/<uuid>/settings/shadow_doorbell
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	linux-nvme@lists.infradead.org
Description:
		(RW) Whether the virtual controller offers the Doorbell Buffer
		Config command (shadow doorbells), 1 or 0, 1 by default.
		Without shadow doorbells the doorbell page is mapped to the
		guest and the I/O thread polls it continuously.  Can only be
		changed while the device is not open (-EBUSY otherwise).

What:		/sys/bus/mdev/devices/<uuid>/settings/poll_timeout_ms
Date:		November 2026
Contact:	linux-nvme@lists.infradead.org
Description:
		(RW) How long, in milliseconds, the I/O thread keeps polling
		without seeing any I/O before it goes idle and waits for a
		doorbell write; 0 means it never goes idle.  500 by default,
		at most 60000.  Only used with shadow doorbells.
